The invisibility of repair networks creates perceived barriers to maintenance that are often exaggerated relative to actual accessibility. When repair services are not visible or easily discoverable, consumers default to replacement assumptions. Making repair networks visible and accessible directly removes perceived friction from repair decisions.

Service network visibility creates competitive advantage where manufacturers credibly demonstrate support infrastructure. Transparency about service availability builds consumer confidence in product longevity claims. Geographic visualization of service networks particularly influences purchase decisions in regions with sparse service infrastructure.